# Form Input Components

Always use the shared form components from `src/components/forms/` for all user inputs. Never write raw `<input>`, `<select>`, or `<textarea>` elements directly in pages or modals.

## Component Map

| Need | Component | File |
|------|-----------|------|
| Text, number, date, email, password | `AppLabledInput` | `AppLabledInput.tsx` |
| Single-select dropdown | `AppLabeledSelectInput` | `AppLabledSelectInput.tsx` |
| Searchable autocomplete | `AppLabledAutocomplete` | `AppLabledAutocomplete.tsx` |
| Multi-line text | `AppLabeledAreaInput` | `AppLabledAreaInput.tsx` |

## Examples

```tsx
// ✅ correct
<AppLabledInput label="Title" value={title} onChange={(e) => setTitle(e.target.value)} required />
<AppLabeledSelectInput label="Status" value={status} options={STATUS_LABELS} onChange={(e) => setStatus(e.target.value)} />
<AppLabledAutocomplete label="Project" options={projects} accessor="name" valueAccessor="id" onSelect={setProject} />
<AppLabeledAreaInput label="Description" value={desc} onChange={(e) => setDesc(e.target.value)} />

// ❌ wrong — raw HTML elements
<input type="text" value={title} onChange={...} />
<select value={status} onChange={...}>...</select>
<textarea value={desc} onChange={...} />
```

## Notes

- `AppLabeledSelectInput` accepts `options: string[]`. If your options have separate values and labels, define a label→value map and store the label in state, converting on submit.
- `AppLabledAutocomplete` uses `accessor` (display field) and `valueAccessor` (id field) to work with object arrays.
- If none of the existing components fit a new use case, add a new component to `src/components/forms/` rather than using a raw element inline.
